{"subjectId":"c","id":"doubly-linked-list","title":"Doubly & Circular Linked Lists","group":"Data Structures & Algorithms in C","order":42,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A doubly linked list stores <code>prev</code> and <code>next</code> pointers, so a node can be removed in O(1) given only a pointer to it and the list can be walked both ways.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\ntypedef struct DNode {\n    int data;\n    struct DNode *prev, *next;\n} DNode;\n\n/* circular list with a sentinel: no NULL checks at the ends */\ntypedef struct { DNode head; size_t size; } List;\n\nvoid list_init(List *l) { l-&gt;head.prev = l-&gt;head.next = &amp;l-&gt;head; l-&gt;size = 0; }\n\nvoid insert_after(List *l, DNode *pos, int v) {\n    DNode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (!n) exit(1);\n    n-&gt;data = v;\n    n-&gt;prev = pos;\n    n-&gt;next = pos-&gt;next;\n    pos-&gt;next-&gt;prev = n;\n    pos-&gt;next = n;\n    l-&gt;size++;\n}\n\nvoid push_front(List *l, int v) { insert_after(l, &amp;l-&gt;head, v); }\nvoid push_back(List *l, int v)  { insert_after(l, l-&gt;head.prev, v); }\n\nvoid unlink_node(List *l, DNode *n) {        /* O(1): no search for the predecessor */\n    n-&gt;prev-&gt;next = n-&gt;next;\n    n-&gt;next-&gt;prev = n-&gt;prev;\n    free(n);\n    l-&gt;size--;\n}\n\nvoid print(const List *l) {\n    for (const DNode *n = l-&gt;head.next; n != &amp;l-&gt;head; n = n-&gt;next) printf(\"%d \", n-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"| backwards: \");\n    for (const DNode *n = l-&gt;head.prev; n != &amp;l-&gt;head; n = n-&gt;prev) printf(\"%d \", n-&gt;data);\n    printf(\"(size %zu)\\n\", l-&gt;size);\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    List l;\n    list_init(&amp;l);\n    push_back(&amp;l, 2); push_back(&amp;l, 3); push_front(&amp;l, 1); push_back(&amp;l, 4);\n    print(&amp;l);\n    unlink_node(&amp;l, l.head.next-&gt;next);       /* remove the 2 */\n    print(&amp;l);\n    while (l.size) unlink_node(&amp;l, l.head.next);\n    print(&amp;l);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 3 4 | backwards: 4 3 2 1 (size 4)\n1 3 4 | backwards: 4 3 1 (size 3)\n| backwards: (size 0)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A <strong>sentinel</strong> (dummy head) node makes the list circular and never empty, which removes every special case for the first and last element. The Linux kernel’s <code>list_head</code> uses this design.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Singly linked</td><td>Smallest nodes, simplest</td><td>Can’t go back; deleting needs the predecessor</td></tr><tr><td>Doubly linked</td><td>O(1) delete of any node, both directions</td><td>Two pointers per node</td></tr><tr><td>Circular</td><td>Round-robin traversal; no NULL end</td><td>Must stop by comparing with the start</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is deletion O(1) in a doubly linked list but O(n) in a singly linked one?</summary><div><p>To unlink a node you must update its predecessor’s <code>next</code>. A doubly linked node knows its predecessor (<code>prev</code>); in a singly linked list you have to walk from the head to find it (unless you already hold it).</p></div></details>"}